id: recipe.local-to-kubernetes
kind: recipe
title: Local development to Kubernetes
aliases: local to kubernetes, dev workflow, parity, inner loop
question: how do you go from a service running on your laptop to running it in Kubernetes?
tags: architecture, workflow, kubernetes
properties:
  - the same artifact runs locally and in the cluster
  - configuration injected, not baked into the image
  - the inner loop is seconds, not minutes
  - cluster-only behaviour caught before the first deploy
invariant: One image, many environments: build the same artifact, inject all configuration, and make the local loop fast enough to run the checks that only the cluster can fail.
answer_30s: Containerise once, keep configuration external (env, ConfigMap, Secret), run the same image locally with the same entrypoint, and rehearse the cluster-only risks -- probes, shutdown, DNS, resource limits -- before the first deploy.
flow: |
  code -> build image (same image for every environment)
     |
  local run: docker/compose or mirrord-style traffic interception into a real pod
     |
  configuration: env + ConfigMap/Secret mounted, never baked in
     |
  pre-deploy rehearsal: readiness probe path, SIGTERM drain, DNS names, resource limits
     |
  deploy: rollout, observe endpoints, rollback if needed
compose:
  - k8s.configmap => environment configuration injected rather than built into the image
  - k8s.secret => credentials never baked into the image or committed
  - k8s.probe.readiness => the health path must exist before the first rollout
  - k8s.probe.liveness => the same for restart behaviour
  - k8s.resources.requests => local "it runs" is not the same as a container with 256Mi
  - k8s.resources.limits => memory limits expose leaks and working-set mistakes locally
  - k8s.dns-service-discovery => service names do not resolve on a laptop; plan for it
  - k8s.port-forward => reach a cluster dependency from local code without exposing it
  - k8s.logs => the first place to look when the deployed container behaves differently
  - k8s.deployment.rolling-update => the deploy path you are rehearsing
  - design.graceful-shutdown => shutdown behaviour is a cluster-only failure mode
  - design.schema-migration => local databases rarely match production schema state
  - recipe.zero-downtime-deploy => the rollout you are about to do
failure_modes:
  - configuration baked into the image, so environments cannot differ safely
  - no readiness endpoint, so the first rollout takes traffic to an unready process
  - local-only assumptions (hostnames, paths, writable filesystem) failing in the cluster
  - resource requests invented at deploy time, so pods are evicted or Pending
  - long bake-and-push loops, so the checks that matter are skipped under time pressure
follow_ups:
  - how would you debug a container that works locally but crashes in the cluster?
related:
  - k8s.configmap
  - k8s.secret
  - k8s.port-forward
  - recipe.zero-downtime-deploy
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-pod-configmap/
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/
